WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:08.760
When most people think of pollen, they
probably think of allergies and runny

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:14.610
noses. While pollen may be the source of
our sneezes. It is also the source of our

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:16.940
food.

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:23.200
Pollen contains sperm,
which fertilizes plants, allowing them to

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:27.599
reproduce. What ends up on our dinner plates begins

00:00:27.599 --> 00:00:30.239
when a pollen grain lands on a plant's
stigma.

00:00:30.239 --> 00:00:35.640
Soon after, the pollen grain germinates
into a pollen tube, which is a true hero

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:41.129
of our story. The pollen tube is an
incredible, single cell focused entirely

00:00:41.129 --> 00:00:45.949
on delivering to sperm cells to the
plant's ovule.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:52.870
To arrive at the ovule, the pollen tube
undergoes tip growth, which is a form of

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:56.310
unidirectional cell growth.

00:00:56.519 --> 00:01:01.539
This is a rather exceptional process,
considering that the cell can grow over

00:01:01.539 --> 00:01:06.940
a thousand times its original length in
just a few hours! In fact, pollen tubes

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:12.100
are the fastest recording extending
cells. After the pollen tube has

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:16.210
traveled the entire length of the plant's
style, it arrives at the ovule, which

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:22.869
contains the egg and central cell. Once
here, the pollen tube bursts and releases

00:01:22.869 --> 00:01:28.210
the two sperm cells, for a process called
double fertilization. One of the sperm

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:32.979
fuses with the egg, creating the embryo
that will become the new plant. The other

00:01:32.979 --> 00:01:37.590
sperm fuses with the central cell, which
develops into the endosperm, a

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:44.140
nutrient-rich tissue that supports the
development of the embryo. This is what

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:49.210
we eventually will eat! Wheat endosperm
is ground into flour for bread, and the

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:53.259
endosperm of corn are the kernels that
we eat directly off the cob.

00:01:53.259 --> 00:01:58.450
These products are global food staples,
and we depend heavily on them in order

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:05.020
to feed our planet's growing population.
As we've seen, the growth of the pollen

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:09.420
tube transports the sperm to the plant's
ovule, where fertilization occurs.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:14.320
Fertilization allows the plant, whether
it's corn, wheat, or another crop to start

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.680
growing.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:22.110
So, the success of the pollen tube's
journey, from stigma to ovule, is

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:26.910
essential for our food supply system. If
this cellular journey goes wrong, we

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:34.110
cannot produce major crops such as corn,
rice, wheat, and tomatoes. The growth of

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:38.460
the pollen tube is temperature-sensitive,
so climate change poses a great risk to

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:43.650
our food supply. Out of all plant cells,
pollen tubes are the most temperature

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:47.940
sensitive. In fact, a pollen tube that is
fully functional at a certain

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:52.260
temperature might be unable to locate
the ovule at a temperature only a few

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:56.970
degrees higher, resulting in a completely
sterile plant!

00:02:56.970 --> 00:03:02.010
For instance, experiments have shown that
the pollen tubes of corn and wheat grown

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:07.200
at 30 degrees Celsius (86 degrees
Fahrenheit) were unable to successfully

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:11.730
complete double fertilization. Climate
scientists predict that global

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:17.960
temperatures may rise between 2 and
6 degrees Celsius by the year 2100.

00:03:18.349 --> 00:03:23.689
So in many places where corn and wheat
are grown, the "threshold temperature" of

00:03:23.689 --> 00:03:28.549
30 degrees Celsius will likely be
crossed by the end of the century. This

00:03:28.549 --> 00:03:33.889
puts us at risk of losing some of our
staple foods. So what can we do to

00:03:33.889 --> 00:03:38.359
protect our food supply? One solution
would be to halt climate change by

00:03:38.359 --> 00:03:43.480
greatly reducing our admission of
greenhouse gases, such as carbon dioxide.

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:48.379
However, at this point, climate change has progressed so far that it may not be

00:03:48.379 --> 00:03:54.980
possible to save our crops. Instead,
scientists are working to engineer

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:58.209
thermotolerant crops

00:03:59.849 --> 00:04:05.400
To do this, more research needs to be
done to understand how each step of the

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:10.849
pollen tube journey could be engineered
to be more heat-resistant.

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:17.769
So the next time you sneeze, or eat a
piece of pizza, be sure to think about

00:04:17.769 --> 00:04:21.820
the mighty pollen tube, and the
scientists working hard to protect its

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:41.139
incredible journey.

